          "caption": "Multiplication 1101 × 110 (13 × 6 = 78)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Two's complement",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.4"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "definition",
          "term": "Two's complement",
          "content": "A way to represent **signed integers** in binary: the most significant bit (MSB) has a **negative place value** (−128 in 8 bits); the other bits are positive."
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "8-bit two's complement place values",
          "columns": [
            "−128",
            "64",
            "32",
            "16",
            "8",
            "4",
            "2",
            "1"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "0",
              "1",
              "1",
              "0",
              "0",
              "0",
              "1"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "10110001 = −128 + 32 + 16 + 1 = **−79**. (As an *unsigned* integer the same byte is 177.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Writing −45 in 8-bit two's complement",
          "items": [
            "Write +45 in binary: **00101101**.",
            "**Invert** every bit: 11010010.",
            "**Add 1**: 11010011 → **−45**.",
            "Check: −128 + 64 + 16 + 2 + 1 = −45 ✓"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "info",
          "title": "Range",
          "content": "With *n* bits, two's complement represents **−2ⁿ⁻¹ to 2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1**. In 8 bits: **−128 to +127**. A positive number starts with 0, a negative number with 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive",
          "widget": "twos_complement",
          "title": "Explore two's complement"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Subtraction using two's complement",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.5"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "To calculate **A − B**, add **A + (−B)** where −B is the two's complement of B; ignore any carry out of the MSB."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "text",
          "content": " 14 = 0000 1110 → invert 1111 0001 → +1 → 1111 0010  (−14)\n\n   0001 0111   (23)\n + 1111 0010   (−14)\n -----------\n 1 0000 1001   → ignore the carry → 0000 1001 = 9 ✓",
          "caption": "23 − 14 in 8 bits"
        },
        {
          "type": "warning",
          "content": "Overflow in two's complement happens when adding two numbers **with the same sign** gives a result with the **opposite sign** (e.g. 100 + 50 in 8 bits gives a negative result)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Fixed-point binary fractions",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.6"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "A fixed number of bits is used for the **integer part** and for the **fractional part**; the binary point is in a fixed position. Fraction place values are **½, ¼, ⅛, 1/16 …**"
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "8 bits: 4 integer bits . 4 fraction bits",
          "columns": [
            "8",
            "4",
            "2",
            "1",
            ".",
            "0.5",
            "0.25",
            "0.125",
            "0.0625"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "1",
              "1",
              "0",
              ".",
              "1",
              "0",
              "1",
              "0"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "0110.1010 = 4 + 2 + 0.5 + 0.125 = **6.625**"
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Byte 10110001 as unsigned fixed point with 5 integer and 3 fraction bits",
          "content": "10110.001 = 16 + 4 + 2 + 0.125 = **22.125**"
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Denary → fixed point: 5.75 with 4.4 bits",
          "items": [
            "Integer part 5 → 0101.",
            "Fraction 0.75 = 0.5 + 0.25 → .1100.",
            "Result: **0101.1100**."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "warning",
          "content": "Some fractions (e.g. 0.1) **cannot be stored exactly** in binary — they are rounded, which causes small errors."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Floating-point representation",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.7"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "definition",
          "term": "Floating point",
          "content": "A number is stored as **mantissa × 2^exponent**. The **mantissa** holds the significant digits (precision); the **exponent** says where the binary point is (range). Both are usually in two's complement."
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Denary → normalised floating point: 17.75 (10-bit mantissa, 6-bit exponent)",
          "items": [
            "17.75 in binary: 10001.11",
            "Move the point so the number is 0.1…: **0.1000111** × 2⁵ (point moved 5 places left).",
            "Mantissa (10 bits, sign bit first): **0100011100**",
            "Exponent 5 in 6 bits: **000101**"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Negative number: −3.5 (8-bit mantissa, 4-bit exponent)",
          "items": [
            "+3.5 = 11.1 = 0.111 × 2² → mantissa 0.1110000, exponent 0010.",
            "Two's complement of the mantissa 01110000 → 10010000.",
            "−3.5 = mantissa **10010000**, exponent **0010**."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "info",
          "title": "Normalisation",
          "content": "A normalised **positive** mantissa starts **0.1**; a normalised **negative** mantissa starts **1.0**. Normalisation gives the **maximum precision** for the bits available and a **unique** representation for each number."
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Mantissa vs exponent bits",
          "columns": [
            "More bits for the mantissa",
            "More bits for the exponent"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Greater **precision** (accuracy)",
              "Greater **range** (larger and smaller numbers)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Floating point → denary",
          "content": "Mantissa 0.1010000, exponent 0011 (= 3): 0.101 × 2³ = 101.0₂ = **5**"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
